{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "aws-multiregion-read-local-write-global-v1",
  "title": "Multi-region read-local, write-primary application resilience",
  "subtitle": "Regional application stacks serve local reads while a designated primary accepts writes and replicates data asynchronously to a recovery region. Synthetic Azure reference; no deployment is implied.",
  "description": "### Problem\nA workload needs regional availability for read-heavy traffic while accepting that cross-region data replication is asynchronous and recovery may require reconciliation.\n\n### Flow and Azure mapping\nGlobal traffic routing sends users to a healthy regional application endpoint. Reads can use local replicas or caches; writes are directed to a selected primary data region. Azure SQL geo-replication or a selected Cosmos DB multi-region configuration may be considered only after confirming the workload's consistency and conflict requirements. A failover procedure changes routing and validates the data state; event replay or reconciliation handles in-flight work.\n\n### Trade-offs and assumptions\nThe source stresses that asynchronous replication can create stale reads and missing or in-flight writes after failure, and that applications often need changes for failover, idempotency, and reconciliation. Azure Cosmos DB and SQL geo-replication have different semantics; neither should be labeled a generic replacement for DynamoDB Global Tables or Aurora. No RTO/RPO or consistency guarantee is claimed.\n\nSource: [AWS Prescriptive Guidance](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/aws-multi-region-fundamentals/fundamental-2.html).\n\n## Source provenance\n\n- Origin provider: AWS\n- Target: Azure conceptual adaptation\n- Primary source: [Multi-Region fundamental 2: Understanding the data](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/aws-multi-region-fundamentals/fundamental-2.html)\n- Reviewed: 2026-11-06\n\n## Catalog status\n\nThis is an original, editable Azure conceptual adaptation. It is synthetic reference material, not a deployed system, certified vendor architecture, or automatic deployment plan.\n\n## Mapping caveats\n\n- Consistency and failover behavior are database-specific; asynchronous replication can lose or delay unreplicated writes.",
